  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Which of the following functions is continuous on the interval $[-1,2]?$",
  "worked_solution": "The correct answer is the following. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945275/assets/e57af2e43bd292195f617f60ffed8055891531a2ca5d0249706497d7932d6639.png)\n\n      \n\nIndeed, this function is continuous on the open intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nAdditionally, the function is left-continuous at $x=2.$ Hence, it is continuous on the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nSo, we can say that the function is continuous on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2]$ and  $(2,\\infty ).$ It is also continuous on any subinterval of these intervals.\n\nTherefore, this function is continuous on $[-1,2],$ because $[-1,2]$ is contained inside the interval $(-\\infty ,2].$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- $g(x)$ and $p(x)$ are not left-continuous at $x=2.$\n\n- $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=-1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$\n\n- $q(x)$ is not defined at $x=1,$ and so it is not continuous on the interval $[-1,2].$",
